(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-cms-exam-7-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 7 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has a fasting glucose of 118 mg/dL (normal 70-99) and triglycerides of 210 mg/dL (normal below 150). Which consequence of her sleep disorder do these results reflect?","choices":["Hypothyroidism caused by the apnea","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Cortisol deficiency from poor sleep","Insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have increased prevalence of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes and diabetes complications, and the disease is independently associated with increased glucose and triglyceride levels. It may also worsen cardiovascular risk in metabolic syndrome. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 20, 21"},{"q":"In a raised-gap acidosis, the change in bicarbonate is larger than the change in anion gap, and it equals the change in anion gap plus a rise in chloride. What does this show?","choices":["A coexisting normal-gap acidosis","A coexisting respiratory alkalosis","A coexisting metabolic alkalosis","A simple raised-gap acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When the change in bicarbonate is accounted for by the change in gap and the change in chloride together, a raised-gap and a normal-gap metabolic acidosis are present at once, as in diarrhea with lactic acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 50"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has had several short nights, and his first recovery night showed short rapid eye movement episodes. He asks what will happen to those episodes as he catches up on sleep. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["They will stay the same","They will get shorter","They will get longer","They will stop occurring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement episode length follows sleepiness: when a person is extremely sleepy each episode is short or even absent, and as the person becomes more rested (less sleepy), the duration of the episodes increases. Catching up on sleep should lengthen his episodes. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman who has never smoked is diagnosed with lung cancer. For 30 years she has lived in a house whose basement tested high for radon, and she has no other known exposures. She asks how she could develop lung cancer without smoking. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Second-hand smoke explains it","Genetics is the sole cause","Lung cancer requires smoking","Radon exposure is a risk factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is implicated in 85 to 90% of lung cancers, not all of them, and radiation and radon exposure are listed among the other risk factors, beside air pollution, environmental and chemical toxins, tuberculosis or human immunodeficiency virus infection, genetics, diet and alcohol. Her 30 years of radon exposure is a recognized cause. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a peripheral lung adenocarcinoma resected. The pathology report describes lepidic growth (tumor cells spreading along the alveolar lining). She asks which lung cells this cancer usually starts from. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Type II pneumocytes","Type I pneumocytes","Neuroendocrine cells","Cells lining the bronchus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Type II pneumocytes are usually the origin of adenocarcinomas, starting with atypical adenomatous hyperplasia and growing first in situ, then enlarging by lepidic growth across the alveolar lining or invading the stroma. Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal, secrete surfactant and make up 60% of alveolar epithelial cells but only 3 to 5% of the surface. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 32, 39, 41"},{"q":"In an elderly patient with suspected pulmonary embolism and a Wells score of 4 or less, what follows from applying the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria)?","choices":["Stop the workup","Proceed straight to imaging","Repeat the Wells score","Obtain a D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) are passed only when all eight are true, and one of them is age under 50, so an elderly patient fails the rule. With the Wells score at 4 or less, a failed rule leads to a D-dimer: a negative result rules embolism out, a positive one proceeds to computed tomographic pulmonary angiography. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has vomited repeatedly for 3 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which effect on oxygen delivery does her acid-base disorder produce?","choices":["Lower hemoglobin affinity; more oxygen delivered","Higher hemoglobin affinity; more oxygen delivered","Lower hemoglobin affinity; less oxygen delivered","Higher hemoglobin affinity; less oxygen delivered"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.48 with a bicarbonate of 34 is a metabolic alkalosis (vomiting loses gastric acid), and the carbon dioxide of 47 is respiratory compensation. Alkalosis increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, so less oxygen is delivered to the tissues. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient with risk factors for pulmonary embolism has no shortness of breath. How should that absence be weighed?","choices":["It excludes a massive clot","Do not rely on it","It rules out embolism","It lowers the Wells score"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Do not rely on shortness of breath: approximately 25% of patients with proven pulmonary embolism completely lack dyspnea. Its absence therefore cannot lower suspicion; the workup still runs through the Wells score and, when embolism is unlikely, the rule-out criteria and D-dimer. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 54, 58"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman who has cut and polished stone for 22 years has silicosis. Her clinician reviews the known complications of silicosis, one of which is a connective tissue disease. Which connective tissue disease is it?","choices":["Scleroderma","Ankylosing spondylitis","Polymyositis-dermatomyositis","Sjögren's syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complications of silicosis are an increased risk of tuberculosis, progressive massive fibrosis and scleroderma. Scleroderma is also one of the connective tissue diseases that produce interstitial lung disease, so new findings in her deserve that thought. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 64"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with obesity hypoventilation syndrome and obstructive sleep apnea has failed a weight loss program and cannot tolerate positive airway pressure. Which second-line therapy is described as very effective?","choices":["Adaptive servo-ventilation","Bariatric surgery for weight loss","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Autotitrating positive airway pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For patients who fail or do not tolerate first-line therapy, second-line options include tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation, which is very effective, and bariatric surgery, which may be helpful. She has failed weight loss and cannot tolerate positive airway pressure, so she qualifies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man smoked 1.5 packs a day for 30 years and quit 22 years ago. He has no symptoms and no known lung nodule, and he asks about lung cancer screening scans. Which describes his eligibility for screening?","choices":["Not eligible: quit too long ago","Not eligible: too old","Eligible on pack-years alone","Eligible for yearly screening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening covers adults 55 to 80 with at least a 30 pack-year history who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. He is 72 with 1.5 packs a day x 30 years = 45 pack-years, but he quit 22 years ago, beyond the 15-year window, so he does not qualify. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 18"},{"q":"Which autonomic change marks non-rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Increased parasympathetic tone","Increased peripheral vascular tone","Rising blood pressure","Irregular heart and breathing rates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep brings increased parasympathetic tone, with low blood pressure, a slow heart rate and a decreased breathing rate. Blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate and metabolic rate fall by 10 to 30%. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven 2.8-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the right upper lobe. Positron emission tomography shows uptake in a hilar lymph node on the same side, but no mediastinal or other nodes are involved, and there is no distant metastasis. Which stage is her cancer?","choices":["Stage 1A","Stage 2A","Stage 2B","Stage 3A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A tumor no more than 5 cm that has spread to the peribronchial, hilar or intrapulmonary nodes is stage 2B. Her 2.8-cm tumor would be stage 1A without nodes, but the involved same-side hilar node, scored N1, moves it to stage 2B; mediastinal nodes would have made it stage 3. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a peripheral cavitating lung mass. Histology shows anaplastic, undifferentiated, pleomorphic cells, with no features of adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma and no specific marker. He asks how his cancer type was decided. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Sputum cytology identified it","A specific marker confirmed it","It is a diagnosis of exclusion","Cavitation on imaging proved it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma lacks specific genetic or histologic markers and is a diagnosis of exclusion among the non-small cell cancers. Its cells are anaplastic, undifferentiated and pleomorphic, which separates it from adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, and histology must confirm the anaplastic tissue even when computed tomography suggests it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 34, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with pneumomediastinum after forceful breath-holding while inhaling an illicit drug develops worsening dyspnea, subcutaneous emphysema spreading up his neck and a falling oxygen saturation despite oxygen. His airway is being supported. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Gastroenterology clinic","Cardiothoracic surgery","Genetics referral","Outpatient pulmonology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pneumomediastinum is usually managed conservatively, but if it is severe, cardiothoracic surgery is consulted emergently, after the airway is assessed and maintained. His worsening dyspnea, spreading emphysema and falling saturation despite oxygen mark severe disease. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 60, 75, 76, 79"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis shows pH 7.17 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 34 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How is the respiratory response best described?","choices":["Appropriate respiratory compensation","Concurrent respiratory acidosis","Concurrent metabolic alkalosis","Concurrent respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28. The measured 34 is above that range. Though 34 is below normal, it is higher than compensation requires, so a concurrent respiratory acidosis is present. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis quit smoking at diagnosis. She asks whether any medicine has been shown to help her live longer. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Corticosteroids reverse the fibrosis","Methotrexate slows the scarring","Surgical biopsy removes the disease","No treatment has improved survival"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is controversial: nothing has been shown to improve survival or quality of life compared with no treatment. Accurate diagnosis helps identify patients who may benefit from available therapies, and her smoking cessation remains critical to prolonging life. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 18, 35, 37, 38"},{"q":"Which disorder results from an organic rather than an inorganic occupational or environmental exposure?","choices":["Silicosis","Asbestosis","Bird fancier's lung","Coal workers' pneumoconiosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The organic exposures that cause interstitial lung disease include bird fancier's lung, farmer's lung, mushroom worker's lung and hot tub lung. Inhaled organic dusts such as bird excreta and feathers cause hypersensitivity pneumonitis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 65"},{"q":"Which change in a nodule over time is concerning?","choices":["Enlarging in size","Shrinking in size","Calcifying centrally","Staying the same size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nodule that is enlarging in size over time is a concerning feature, together with size over 6 mm, upper lobe or hilar location, a spiculated border, more than three nodules, contrast enhancement and a cavitated or nonsolid appearance. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"A 4-month-old boy who is feeding well and developing normally has an electroencephalogram showing high-voltage waves below 3.5 cycles per second. Which explanation best fits this finding?","choices":["A comatose state","Serious organic brain disease","Emotional stress","A normal finding in infancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-voltage waves below 3.5 cycles per second are delta waves, and infancy is one of the settings where delta waves occur, along with very deep sleep and serious organic brain disease. In a healthy infant who is feeding well and developing normally, infancy itself explains them. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 5, 25"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has interstitial lung disease, and a lung biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with vasculitis. His cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody test is positive. Which disease does this result point to?","choices":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Goodpasture syndrome","Sarcoidosis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A positive cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis, one of the granulomatous vasculitides that produce interstitial lung disease with a granulomatous response. His biopsy showing granulomas with vasculitis fits that pairing. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 25, 44, 69"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction takes all four classes of quadruple therapy at maximal tolerated levels. He is breathless at rest and has been hospitalized four times in 6 months. Which step fits now?","choices":["Continue and recheck in a year","Add a fifth oral drug class","Stage C maintenance unchanged","Evaluation for advanced therapies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Marked symptoms at rest despite maximal therapy, with frequent hospitalizations, define stage D heart failure. Stage D calls for immediate evaluation for advanced therapies: heart transplantation, mechanical circulatory support such as a left ventricular assist device, or palliative and hospice care. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 41, 44"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has no signs of right ventricular failure and no syncope, and her symptoms have progressed slowly over 2 years. She is in World Health Organization functional class II, and her six-minute walk distance is 470 m. Which prognostic risk band fits her?","choices":["Low risk, under 5% at 1 year","Low risk, 5-10% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, 5-10% at 1 year","High risk, over 10% at 1 year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every feature sits in the low-risk row: no right ventricular failure signs, slow progression, no syncope, functional class II and a six-minute walk distance above 440 m. Low risk carries an estimated 1-year mortality under 5%. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has episodes of rapid breathing and palpitations when she is anxious; her examination is otherwise normal. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 32 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Faster breathing will help clear the episodes","Slow, mindful breathing can ease the episodes","Daily potassium supplements prevent the episodes","She will need mechanical ventilation if they recur"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.48 with a low carbon dioxide is a mild respiratory alkalosis from anxiety. With a pH below 7.50 treatment is usually not required; treating the anxiety and mindful breathing to control the hyperventilation are the measures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 39, 40"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breaths. On auscultation there is a grating, creaking friction rub over the lower anterior chest, and the clinician is unsure whether it arises from the pleura or the pericardium. Which bedside maneuver best separates the two?","choices":["Having him cough","Palpation for fremitus","Percussion for dullness","Breath holding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pleural friction rub disappears when the patient holds his breath, and that is the feature that differentiates it from a pericardial friction rub. The pleural rub comes from inflamed pleural layers rubbing together during respiration, so stopping respiration stops the sound. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 22, 87, 88"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman lies relaxed with her eyes closed at the start of a sleep study, and her electroencephalogram shows alpha waves. What happens to these alpha waves once she reaches deep sleep?","choices":["They persist unchanged","They disappear","They grow larger","They speed up into beta"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alpha waves belong to quiet, relaxed wakefulness, such as resting with the eyes closed, and they disappear during deep sleep. As she sinks into deep non-rapid eye movement sleep the tracing slows progressively, until delta waves below 3.5 cycles per second take over in stage N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 26, 27, 24, 28"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with a 35 pack-year history has 2 months of persistent cough and hemoptysis. A chest film shows a 2-cm opacity in the right upper lobe. Which next study is least useful in her workup?","choices":["Metabolic panel with calcium","Contrast chest computed tomography","Biopsy guided by nodule location","Repeat chest radiograph"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A chest film is not a good tool for working up a suspicious nodule or symptoms: it may be the first image to show a nodule, but otherwise it serves to rule out other pathology. Her symptoms and opacity call for computed tomography with contrast of the chest and upper abdomen, then biopsy, with laboratory studies. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and chronic carbon dioxide retention has an exacerbation. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 74 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 37 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His partial pressure of oxygen is 52 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100 mm Hg). Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Giving oxygen in carefully titrated amounts","Reviewing his medicines for sedatives","Driving his carbon dioxide down rapidly","Treating the cause of the exacerbation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His bicarbonate of 37 shows chronic renal compensation. Treatment is similar to acute disease but with careful oxygen, because a rapidly falling carbon dioxide in chronic respiratory acidosis may trigger the complications of alkalosis: cardiac arrhythmias, reduced cerebral perfusion and seizures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 36, 37"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with limited-stage small cell lung carcinoma has a complete response to initial chemotherapy and thoracic radiation. Her cognition is normal and she is active and independent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Prophylactic cranial irradiation","Routine positron emission tomography","Observation alone","Lobectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After initial therapy, limited-stage small cell carcinoma with a complete or partial response goes on to prophylactic cranial irradiation, radiating the brain to prevent metastasis. Her normal cognition and good function leave no reason to withhold it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 41, 43"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has exertional dyspnea and signs of right ventricular failure. His chest film and pulmonary function tests are normal, a ventilation-perfusion scan shows several mismatched segmental perfusion defects, and he had a pulmonary embolism 2 years ago. Which World Health Organization pulmonary hypertension group fits?","choices":["Group 1","Group 2","Group 3","Group 4"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal lung studies rule out lung disease (Group 3), and segmental perfusion defects after a past embolism point to unresolved clot burden. That is chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension, the obstructive pre-capillary Group 4. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 12, 19"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction develops pneumonia with sepsis, then bilateral alveolar infiltrates and severe hypoxemia. His echocardiogram is technically limited, and his B-type natriuretic peptide is moderately raised. The team cannot tell whether his edema is cardiogenic or from a leaking barrier. Which test settles the question?","choices":["Pulmonary artery catheter wedge pressure","A repeat chest film in 6 hours","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","Serial natriuretic peptide levels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pulmonary artery catheter (right heart catheterization) is the ultimate hemodynamic arbiter: it directly measures left-sided filling pressure when the cause remains uncertain. The wedge looks forward to the left atrium; above 18-20 mmHg confirms cardiogenic failure, while a normal value points to a permeability leak. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 33, 36, 38"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman in the intensive care unit has septic acute kidney injury (creatinine 3.1 mg/dL, normal 0.6-1.1). She develops sudden hypoxemia and a swollen right leg, pulmonary embolism is judged likely, and she is too unstable to leave the unit. Which imaging fits her?","choices":["A D-dimer to decide on imaging","Transthoracic echocardiography alone","Portable bedside perfusion scan","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ventilation-perfusion approach is best for renal dysfunction, severe contrast allergy and critically ill patients, who can have a portable bedside perfusion scan. Her kidney injury argues against contrast, and her instability argues against travel to a scanner. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 55, 56"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, takes aspirin daily, takes isoniazid for latent tuberculosis, and has had profuse watery diarrhea for 5 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of her history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Metformin","Her diarrhea","Aspirin","Isoniazid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 138 - (15 + 114) = 9, normal, with a high chloride. Diarrhea, gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss, is one of the most common normal-gap causes. Metformin, aspirin and isoniazid all raise the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 46, 47"},{"q":"Below what ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen is the oxygenation criterion for acute respiratory distress syndrome met?","choices":["100 mm Hg","200 mm Hg","300 mm Hg","400 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oxygenation impairment threshold is a ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen under 300 mm Hg (or a ratio of arterial oxygen saturation to fraction of inspired oxygen under 315). Within it, 200 to 300 is mild, 100 to 200 moderate and under 100 severe. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with uremia from kidney failure has vomited for 5 days and is dry on examination. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.41 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 41 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 25 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 143 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 97 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Give bicarbonate to treat the acidosis","Treat the vomiting alone","Replace fluids and treat the kidney failure","No treatment since his pH is normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 143 - (97 + 25) = 21, change in gap = 11, change in bicarbonate = 0: a uremic acidosis with a metabolic alkalosis from vomiting. Both causes need treatment: replace his fluid deficit for the vomiting and treat the uremia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 63, 30, 54, 23"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with months of poor oral intake is admitted with a new infection. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 35 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 106 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her albumin is 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). What is her anion gap after correction for her albumin?","choices":["12","14.5","17","19.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The measured gap is 136 - (18 + 106) = 12. The correction adds 2.5 for each 1 g/dL that albumin sits below 4.5: 4.5 - 2.5 = 2, and 2 x 2.5 = 5, so the corrected gap is 12 + 5 = 17, above the normal 6 to 12. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 57"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has a primary spontaneous pneumothorax. His respiratory rate is 28 breaths per minute, heart rate 104 beats per minute, blood pressure 124/78 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 95% on room air, and he speaks in whole sentences. How is he classified by the American College of Chest Physicians stability definition?","choices":["Stable","Unstable: respiratory rate","Unstable: oxygen saturation","Unstable: heart rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stability requires all of: respiratory rate under 24 breaths per minute, heart rate over 60 and under 120, normal blood pressure, room-air saturation over 90% and whole sentences. His heart rate of 104, normal pressure, saturation of 95% and full sentences pass, but a rate of 28 fails, and every other patient is unstable. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman being treated with antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia still has fever and pleuritic pain after several days, and her parapneumonic effusion has grown. Thoracentesis returns thick purulent fluid. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A longer antibiotic course","Chemical pleurodesis","Diuretic therapy","Drainage plus antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Empyema is purulent fluid in the pleural space, and bacterial pneumonia with a parapneumonic effusion is its most common precursor. It is suspected when pneumonia does not resolve with therapy and diagnosed by purulent material on thoracentesis; treatment is drainage and antibiotics, treating the underlying disease and analgesia, and prompt therapy is critical. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 97, 107, 108"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with an inherited bleeding disorder has a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax with an apex-to-cupola distance of 2 cm. She still has significant dyspnea after adequate analgesia, a situation in which catheter aspiration is usually offered. Which feature of her history contraindicates catheter aspiration?","choices":["Her bleeding disorder","Her first-episode status","Her pneumothorax size","Her ongoing dyspnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A small primary spontaneous pneumothorax, under 3 cm at the apex, with significant dyspnea after analgesia is treated with catheter aspiration. Bleeding disorders are on the list of contraindications to aspiration, so her inherited bleeding disorder removes the option. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 54"},{"q":"A pleural fluid has a protein ratio to serum of 0.7, and both lactate dehydrogenase criteria are unmet. How is it classified?","choices":["Indeterminate","Normal pleural fluid","Exudate","Transudate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Light's criteria classify a fluid as an exudate when at least one criterion is met, and a protein ratio of 0.7 is above the 0.5 cutoff. The two unmet lactate dehydrogenase criteria do not change that, because a transudate requires all of the criteria to be absent. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"A 47-year-old farmer recovered from acute farmer's lung after a month away from work, and he plans to return to the farm. During his illness he used oxygen, inhaled bronchodilators and pain medication. Which should he avoid?","choices":["Pain medication","Handling moldy hay","Inhaled bronchodilators","Supplemental oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of hypersensitivity pneumonitis is to identify the responsible agent and avoid it, and moldy hay is the source of farmer's lung. Continued exposure may lead to a worsened disease state, while symptoms caught early can be reversible. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 69, 71"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease had a chest film 5 years ago showing fine reticular, ground-glass opacities. Her new film shows medium reticular opacities described as honeycombing. How should this change be classified?","choices":["Healing from active to inactive disease","Progression from early to late disease","A shift to nodular disease","Progression to coarse cystic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among reticular opacities, fine ground-glass change is an early finding and medium reticular change, honeycombing, is a late finding. Moving from ground glass to honeycombing over 5 years marks progression, consistent with fibrosis, which damages the alveoli permanently. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a 60 pack-year history is brought to the emergency department with new confusion, an unsteady gait and a seizure. Serum sodium is 118 mEq/L (normal 135-145) and serum osmolality is 250 mOsm/kg (normal 275-295). He has no peripheral edema and no signs of pulmonary edema. Computed tomography shows a central hilar mass. Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Bronchial carcinoid","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell lung cancer can produce the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone through its neuroendocrine cells, causing excess vasopressin and water retention. The result is hyponatremia and low serum osmolality, here 118 and 250, with confusion, seizures and ataxia but no peripheral or pulmonary edema, because the process is insidious. The central mass fits too. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with weeks of vomiting from a gastric outlet obstruction has muscle weakness and hyporeflexia (diminished reflexes). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis with hyperkalemia","Metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia","Respiratory alkalosis with low potassium","Respiratory acidosis from muscle weakness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.50 with a bicarbonate of 36 is a metabolic alkalosis; vomiting causes it by loss of gastric acid. The carbon dioxide of 48 is compensation. Hypokalemia, partly a shift into cells, causes the weakness and hyporeflexia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 51, 52, 53, 58"},{"q":"A 21-year-old tall, thin man is recovering from his first primary spontaneous pneumothorax. He smokes a pack of cigarettes a day, and his father had the same condition. Before discharge, the clinician counsels him on the risk factor he can change. Which risk factor should the counseling address?","choices":["Family history","Strenuous exercise","Cigarette smoking","Tall, thin build"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is a significant risk factor for primary spontaneous pneumothorax and the one he can change. Recurrence approaches 50% within three years of a first episode, so education includes the importance of smoking cessation and help to quit if he is willing. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 59, 60"},{"q":"Without a qualifying symptom, how many predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour of sleep confirm obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["5 or more","10 or more","15 or more","20 or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fifteen or more predominantly obstructive events per hour confirm obstructive sleep apnea on their own, provided the findings are not explained by another condition, medication or substance. With 5 or more events, a qualifying symptom is also required. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 66-year-old retired coal miner with coal workers' pneumoconiosis and chronic dyspnea asks what can be done for his lungs. He already uses home oxygen and stopped smoking years ago. Which measure belongs in his care?","choices":["A curative antifibrotic drug","Avoiding moldy hay and birds","Early prone positioning","Pneumococcal, influenza vaccines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis has no effective curative treatment, so care is supportive: oxygen, pneumococcal and influenza vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, with lung transplant for severe cases. With oxygen in place and smoking stopped, vaccination is the supportive measure still to add. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 71, 74"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has had pulmonary sarcoidosis for 15 years. His chest film now shows advanced fibrosis with honeycombing, hilar retraction and bullae. Which radiographic stage is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage IV is advanced fibrosis with evidence of honeycombing, hilar retraction, bullae, cysts and emphysema, which his film shows. About 20% of patients with lung involvement experience irreversible lung impairment from progressive fibrosis, bronchiectasis and cavitation. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 45, 48"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman snores loudly. She feels rested during the day, no one has seen her stop breathing, and she is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 36 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 14 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 1, low risk","Score 2, low risk","Score 2, high risk","Score 3, intermediate risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + pressure 0 + body mass index 36 (more than 35) 1 + age 35 0 + neck 14 inches 0 + female 0 = 2, the low-risk band (0 to 2). The body mass index rule needs 2 or more yes answers to the first 4 questions, and she has 1, so she stays low risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man has had profuse watery diarrhea for 4 days and is breathing deeply. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does his carbon dioxide of 30 represent?","choices":["Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis","Carbon dioxide retention from hypoventilation","A primary respiratory alkalosis","Renal compensation for respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A low pH with a low bicarbonate is a metabolic acidosis, and diarrhea is a common cause. When the cause is metabolic, compensation is respiratory: increased breathing lowers carbon dioxide, which pulls the pH toward normal without fully correcting it. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 41, 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man did demolition work around asbestos insulation for 20 years. He now has a pleural effusion, and mesothelioma is suspected. Which test can establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Pleural fluid cytology","Serum antinuclear antibody","Tuberculosis skin test","Bronchoalveolar lavage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mesothelioma, a primarily pleural cancer nearly always caused by asbestos exposure, frequently produces pleural effusions, and it can be diagnosed from pleural fluid cytology. His long asbestos exposure and new effusion make the fluid the sample to examine. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 56, 59, 62, 64"},{"q":"Which lung cancer has the strongest association with smoking?","choices":["Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma, the second most common lung cancer, has the strongest association with smoking. It is central, often arises in a bronchus and shows frequent cavitation and obstruction, with cough, wheezing and hemoptysis once the bronchus is blocked. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 33, 39, 41"},{"q":"In a tension pneumothorax, what is done immediately if a chest tube is not readily available?","choices":["Heimlich valve","Needle decompression","Chest radiograph first","Catheter aspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immediate chest tube placement is ideal for a tension pneumothorax; if a chest tube is not readily available, immediate needle decompression is performed, followed by a moderate or large-size chest tube and admission. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 40, 65, 66, 67"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis has tense ascites and a new right pleural effusion. He has no orthopnea, leg edema or known heart disease, and no fever or cough. Pleural fluid protein is 1.2 g/dL with serum protein 5.8 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 70 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 180 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). Which is the most likely cause of the effusion?","choices":["Pneumonia","Cirrhosis","Heart failure","Tuberculosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 1.2 / 5.8 = 0.21, lactate dehydrogenase ratio 70 / 180 = 0.39, and fluid lactate dehydrogenase 70 is under two-thirds of 280, about 187: all of Light's criteria are absent, a transudate. Cirrhosis with ascites sends peritoneal fluid across the diaphragm and lowers oncotic pressure. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 101, 103, 110, 111"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman develops sudden severe dyspnea 8 days after a hip fracture repair, and computed tomographic pulmonary angiography shows a massive pulmonary embolism. Her blood pressure is 84/52 mmHg and her heart rate 132/min. Which bedside finding is expected from her acutely dilated right ventricle?","choices":["Distended neck veins","Bibasilar crackles","Slow, bounding pulse","Tender hepatomegaly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When the right ventricle dilates acutely, blood backs up behind it and the neck veins distend; a stretched tricuspid annulus adds a regurgitant murmur. Tachycardia and pallor complete the acute picture, which is what her massive embolism and low blood pressure predict. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 10, 11, 23"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a new right pleural effusion. For 2 months he has had fevers, drenching night sweats, hemoptysis and a 6 kg weight loss. He has no leg swelling or orthopnea and no purulent sputum. Which is the most likely cause of his effusion?","choices":["Malignancy","Tuberculosis","Heart failure","Pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fever, night sweats, hemoptysis and weight loss together are the extrapulmonary clue set that points to tuberculosis in a patient with an effusion. Tuberculosis is also the leading cause of exudative effusion worldwide. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 83, 86, 103"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with a confirmed pulmonary embolism becomes confused and clammy. Her blood pressure has been 76/48 mmHg for 40 minutes despite fluids. She has had no recent surgery, no bleeding and no stroke. How is her embolism risk-stratified, and what follows for treatment?","choices":["Massive; systemic thrombolysis","Low risk; outpatient anticoagulation","Massive; anticoagulation alone","Submassive; anticoagulation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sustained hypotension (here 76/48 mmHg for 40 minutes) defines massive, high-risk embolism, a circulatory collapse with high mortality that requires immediate advanced intervention. Thrombolysis is the standard for massive, hemodynamically unstable embolism, and she has no stated contraindication. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, functional class II. During right heart catheterization her pulmonary pressures fall markedly on vasoreactivity testing, so she is vasoreactive. Which therapy fits her?","choices":["Lung transplantation referral","Continuous intravenous prostanoid","Early oral combination therapy","High-dose oral calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasoreactivity testing splits the treatment path: patients who respond are treated with high-dose oral calcium channel blockers. Patients who are not vasoreactive are the ones who move on to risk stratification and the targeted pulmonary arterial hypertension classes. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has trouble falling asleep and asks for a sleeping pill. Which option should be avoided?","choices":["Positional therapy with a sleep shirt","A sedative sleeping pill","Referral to a sleep physician","Diet and exercise for weight loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and the risk modification is to avoid alcohol and other sedatives. A sedative sleeping pill would add a risk factor to his untreated apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 15, 12"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman in a sleep study has an electroencephalogram resembling wakefulness with irregular, sharply peaked eye movements. What is expected of her limb muscle tone during this period?","choices":["Higher than when awake","The same as when awake","Markedly depressed","Kept in limbs, lost in eyes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her waking-like tracing and irregular, sharply peaked eye movements place her in rapid eye movement sleep, in which muscle tone is very depressed: the voluntary muscles show atonia, sparing the eye muscles and diaphragm, from inhibition of spinal muscle control areas and alpha motor neurons. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 28"}]};
